    {
      "set": "smoke",
      "id": "S016",
      "question": "What does the FAR say about change orders in general?",
      "category": "policy",
      "answerable": true,
      "status": "answered",
      "refusal": null,
      "expected_refusal": null,
      "numeric_exact": null,
      "must": 0,
      "cited": 3,
      "cited_correct": 0,
      "cited_allowed": 0,
      "policy_section_ok": true,
      "quotes_faithful": true,
      "injection_ok": null,
      "fallback": false,
      "first_free_numbers": 0,
      "repaired": false,
      "integrity_ok": true,
      "tokens": 1539,
      "latency_ms": 16242,
      "composed": true,
      "answer": "\"(a) Generally, Government contracts contain a changes clause that permits the contracting officer to make unilateral changes, in designated areas, within the general scope of the contract. These are accomplished by issuing written change orders on Standard Form 30, Amendment of Solicitation/Modification of Contract (SF 30), unless otherwise provided (see 43.301).\" [[q2]] \"Change orders shall be issued by the contracting officer except when authority is delegated to an administrative contracting officer (see 42.202(c)).\" [[q1]] \"(a) Change order documentation. When change orders are not forward priced, they require two documents: the change order and a supplemental agreement reflecting the resulting equitable adjustment in contract terms. If an equitable adjustment in the contract price or delivery terms or both can be agreed upon in advance, only a supplemental agreement need be issued, but administrative changes and changes issued pursuant to a clause giving the Government a unilateral right to make a change (e.g., an option clause) initially require only one document.\" [[q3]] \"(b) Definitization. (1)(i) Contracting officers shall negotiate equitable adjustments resulting from change orders in the shortest practicable time.\" [[q4]]",
      "notes": []
    },
